// ============================================================
// 3D-ICE Floorplan: die_top_S_v2.flp
// Stratum-S v2 -- TOP die tier (closest to the TIM / cold plate)
//
// WHAT CHANGED vs v1 (logic_die_S.flp / mem_die_S.flp):
//   1. GPU0 MOVED FROM THE BOTTOM TIER TO THE TOP TIER.
//      Physically the xPU backside faces the cold plate directly; there is no
//      Mono3D DRAM stacked over the GPU. v1 put 151 um of fictitious silicon
//      between GPU0 and the TIM. Worth only ~0.7 K today, but it hard-coded a
//      topology that breaks as soon as more chips are composed onto the panel.
//   2. 2000 um GUARD GAP between the xPU and the Mono3D column.
//      3D-ICE assigns material PER LAYER, not per floorplan element, so every
//      FILL_* block is silicon. In v1 GPU0 and the Mono3D column were directly
//      adjacent, so that fictitious silicon acted as a real lateral short.
//      Die-layer spreading length is ~0.73 mm; a 2 mm gap is ~2.7x that, which
//      bounds the artefact. Real chip-to-chip coupling now goes through the
//      cold plate, which is the correct physics.
//   3. GPU0 resized to 22600 x 27800 = 628.28 mm^2.
//      NVIDIA GA102 whitepaper: 628.4 mm^2. Error 0.02% (v1 was 0.10%).
//      Aspect 1.230 vs David's die-shot estimate 1.240. Also keeps every edge
//      on a 200 um grid so the model can run at 200 um cells.
//   4. Mono3D tile resized to 11000 x 11000 = 121.00 mm^2.
//      Stratum Table 1: "Chip Area 121 mm^2". Exact match (v1 was 120.75).
//   5. GPU0 power 230 W, not 300 W.
//      300 W is the RTX A6000 MAXIMUM BOARD POWER for the whole card, which
//      carries 48 GB of ECC GDDR6 on a 384-bit bus plus VRM and blower.
//      The GA102 die alone is ~200-240 W. See the 300 W worst-case line below.
//
// Canvas: 39600 x 31800 um = 1259.3 mm^2   (v1 was 46600 x 34500 = 1607.7 mm^2)
//   Active fill factor 59.5% (v1: 46.6%) -- less fictitious silicon.
//
// Layout (x):  0-2000 margin | 2000-13000 Mono3D | 13000-15000 GAP
//              | 15000-37600 xPU | 37600-39600 margin
//
// Power density:
//   GPU0 : 230 W / 628.28 mm^2 = 36.6 W/cm^2   (300 W -> 47.8 W/cm^2)
//   MEM0 : 102 W / 121.00 mm^2 = 84.3 W/cm^2
//   Mono3D column total (MEM0 + LOGIC0 below) = 144.67 W / 121 mm^2 = 119.6 W/cm^2
//
// NOTE: die_bot_S_v2.flp uses IDENTICAL block boundaries so Tmap outputs from
//       the two tiers are cell-aligned and can be differenced directly.
// ============================================================

// ---- left margin ----

FILL_LEFT :
    position  0, 0 ;
    dimension 2000, 31800 ;
    power values 0 ;

// ---- Mono3D column: below the tile ----

FILL_M3D_BOT :
    position  2000, 0 ;
    dimension 11000, 10400 ;
    power values 0 ;

// ---- Mono3D DRAM tile (active) ----

MEM0 :
    position  2000, 10400 ;
    dimension 11000, 11000 ;
    power values 102 ;
    // Stratum Fig. 15(b): peak stack 144.53 W, DRAM = 70.5% -> 101.9 W.
    // Section 6.2.2 quotes ~104 W at full 30.34 TB/s bandwidth utilisation.
    // 102 W = the peak-performance operating point. State which one in the paper.

// ---- Mono3D column: above the tile ----

FILL_M3D_TOP :
    position  2000, 21400 ;
    dimension 11000, 10400 ;
    power values 0 ;

// ---- GUARD GAP between the two chips (see header note 2) ----

FILL_GAP :
    position  13000, 0 ;
    dimension 2000, 31800 ;
    power values 0 ;

// ---- xPU column: below GPU0 ----

FILL_GPU_BOT :
    position  15000, 0 ;
    dimension 22600, 2000 ;
    power values 0 ;

// ---- A6000-class GA102 xPU (active) ----

GPU0 :
    position  15000, 2000 ;
    dimension 22600, 27800 ;
    power values 230 ;
    // 22.6 x 27.8 mm = 628.28 mm^2 vs NVIDIA's published 628.4 mm^2 (0.02%).
    // 230 W = GA102 DIE power estimate. The A6000's 300 W is board TBP.
    // WORST CASE: change to 300 and re-run; q" goes 36.6 -> 47.8 W/cm^2.

// ---- xPU column: above GPU0 ----

FILL_GPU_TOP :
    position  15000, 29800 ;
    dimension 22600, 2000 ;
    power values 0 ;

// ---- right margin ----

FILL_RIGHT :
    position  37600, 0 ;
    dimension 2000, 31800 ;
    power values 0 ;
